### Bad Examples

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - No loading states
const fetchData = async () => {
  const data = await api.getData();
  setData(data);
};
*Fails: No loading state management, poor user experience.*

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Poor error handling
const fetchData = async () => {
  try {
    const data = await api.getData();
    setData(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error); // Silent failure
  }
};
*Fails: Silent error handling, no user feedback, no recovery options.*

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Race conditions
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData().then(setData);
}, [dependency]); // No cleanup
*Fails: Potential race conditions, memory leaks, no request cancellation.*

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Unnecessary re-fetching
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [data]); // Infinite loop
*Fails: Infinite re-rendering loop, poor performance, unnecessary network requests.*

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - No caching
const fetchExpensiveData = async () => {
  return await heavyApiCall(); // Called every time
};
*Fails: Poor performance, unnecessary network requests, no optimization.*

```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - Blocking UI
const handleSubmit = async () => {
  await longRunningOperation(); // No loading state
  navigate('/success');
};
*Fails: Blocking UI, poor user experience, no feedback during operation.*
```

## Implementation Guidelines

### Data Loading Checklist

#### Before Implementation
- [ ] Data loading strategy is planned (immediate, lazy, on-demand)
- [ ] Error handling strategy is defined
- [ ] Caching requirements are identified
- [ ] Loading states are designed

#### During Development
- [ ] All async operations have loading states
- [ ] Error handling is comprehensive with user-friendly messages
- [ ] Race conditions are prevented with cleanup
- [ ] Caching is implemented where appropriate
- [ ] Request deduplication is used for identical requests

#### Before Code Review
- [ ] Loading states provide meaningful feedback
- [ ] Error states allow for recovery actions
- [ ] Performance is optimized with caching and batching
- [ ] Memory leaks are prevented with proper cleanup
- [ ] TypeScript types are properly defined

#### Performance Checklist
- [ ] Unnecessary requests are eliminated
- [ ] Data is cached appropriately
- [ ] Large datasets use pagination or virtualization
- [ ] Network requests are optimized and batched
- [ ] Stale-while-revalidate pattern is used where beneficial

### Forbidden Patterns

#### ❌ AVOID These Anti-Patterns
```typescript
// ❌ WRONG - No loading states
const fetchData = async () => {
  const data = await api.getData();
  setData(data);
};

// ❌ WRONG - Poor error handling
const fetchData = async () => {
  try {
    const data = await api.getData();
    setData(data);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(error); // Silent failure
  }
};

// ❌ WRONG - Race conditions
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData().then(setData);
}, [dependency]); // No cleanup

// ❌ WRONG - Unnecessary re-fetching
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [data]); // Infinite loop

// ❌ WRONG - No caching
const fetchExpensiveData = async () => {
  return await heavyApiCall(); // Called every time
};

// ❌ WRONG - Blocking UI
const handleSubmit = async () => {
  await longRunningOperation(); // No loading state
  navigate('/success');
};
```

